1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
Turn these 10 concepts in 2 minutes and you will understand the foundation of every modern

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:08,400
LLM. The same foundation behind chargeability and 300 billion AI industry.

3
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:12,880
Number 1. Tokenization. An LLM does not read full sentences like humans. It breaks it down into

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:18,320
small units called tokens. Token's can be words, part of words, numbers, space and punctuation.

5
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:23,520
So your prompt first becomes a sequence of tokens. Number 2. Embeddings. Each token is converted

6
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:28,400
into vectors. A vector is just a list of numbers. Think of embeddings as coordinates in a meaning

7
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:33,280
space. Similar meanings end up closer together in the vector space. Number 3. Positional

8
00:00:33,280 --> 00:00:38,320
encoding. The model also needs to know the order of tokens because dog bites man and man bites

9
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:42,320
dog have same tokens effectively different meaning. So positional encoding adds positional

10
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:46,160
information. Basically it tells this is token 1, this is token 2, this game before that.

11
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:50,640
Modern models often use methods like rope or rotatory positional embeddings to handle this.

12
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:56,080
Fourth. The transformer block. Now these vectors are entered into transformer. A transformer is a

13
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:59,920
stack of repeated processing layers. Each layer refines the representation of the text. Number 5.

14
00:00:59,920 --> 00:01:04,239
The self-attention mechanism. Self-attention decides which token should influence each other.

15
00:01:04,239 --> 00:01:07,680
It helps the model connect instructions, context, grammar and relationships.

16
00:01:08,320 --> 00:01:12,720
Technically attention uses queries, keys and values. Query means what I am looking for. The key

17
00:01:12,720 --> 00:01:17,040
means what information exists and the value means what information should I use. Number 6. Feed

18
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,400
forward network. After the attention the data goes through several neural networks inside the

19
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:24,400
transformer block. This is where the model transforms the patterns, meanings and relationships into

20
00:01:24,400 --> 00:01:29,520
richer representations. Number 7. Logits. At the end the model produces raw score scored

21
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:34,000
logits. Each logic is a score for a possible next token. For example, Palacemeix score higher,

22
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:39,120
Londonmeix score lower and Banana Avery low score. Number 8. Softmax. Softmax converts logic

23
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:43,200
into probabilities. Think of it as a score to percentage converter. It makes all possible

24
00:01:43,200 --> 00:01:48,080
next tokens probabilities adding up to 100%. So Palace might be 92% London 7% and Banana 1%.

25
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:52,000
Number 9. Stampling. The model then selects the next token. Sometimes it picks the higher

26
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:54,960
probability token. Sometimes it samples from the probability distribution using setting

27
00:01:54,960 --> 00:01:58,720
stack temperature or top P. That's why same prompt can produce slightly different answers

28
00:01:58,720 --> 00:02:03,120
in the locations. Number 10. Training versus inference. Training is when modelers.

29
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:06,560
It predicts tokens, calculates error using a loss function and updates its weights using a

30
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,720
back propagation or a gradient descent. Inference, on other hand, is when you use a trained model.

31
00:02:10,720 --> 00:02:14,400
The weights are frozen. Your prompt becomes a temporary context. The model generates the output

32
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:18,480
one token at a time based on your prompt. So the full pipeline is Texas converted to tokens and

33
00:02:18,480 --> 00:02:22,560
embeddings. And then they are positionally encoded, which goes through a transformer block,

34
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,880
where self-attention mechanism finds the important weights converted to logics, goes through a soft

35
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:31,280
MAC function and sampling happens to generate the next token. That loop repeats again and again

36
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:36,160
until the answer is complete. An LLM isn't magic. It's a transformer repeatedly predicting the

37
00:02:36,160 --> 00:02:40,080
next token billions of times over massive amounts of data. Once you understand this pipeline,

38
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:43,920
every advanced AI concept starts to click. Comment LLM and I'll share some resources to get you.

